package handlers

import (
	"crypto/sha256"
	"encoding/hex"
	"log"
	"net"
	"net/http"
	"strings"

	"gova/app/models"
)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RATE-LIMIT BUCKET KEYS FOR THE TWO LOGIN ENDPOINTS.
//
// `rate_limits` is keyed on one column, `ClearAttempts` is
// `DELETE FROM rate_limits WHERE ip = ?`, and a SUCCESSFUL login calls it. So
// THE KEY IS THE ISOLATION. Two actions that spell the key the same way are one
// action as far as the limiter is concerned, and a success on either erases the
// failures of both.
//
// `/api/v1/auth/login` and `/api/v1/auth/login_token` are the same credential
// check reached two ways — cookie for the browser, bearer for the mobile app —
// and both used to key on a bare IP. The bypass that followed is not
// theoretical and does not need the lock to trip:
//
//	4 wrong passwords against any account  -> attempts = 4, no lock
//	1 correct login_token with an account
//	  the attacker legitimately holds      -> ClearAttempts, the ROW IS GONE
//	4 more wrong passwords                 -> attempts = 4 again
//	  ...
//
// Unbounded guessing at four per round, forever, and the fifth attempt is never
// reached — so the limiter never records a lock, never logs, and reports itself
// healthy the whole time. Pacing at four rather than five is what an attacker
// does because at five the shared lock refuses login_token as well; the shared
// bucket is what makes both halves of that work. Measured in a project built
// from this template, driving the real chi route table: 40 of 40 guesses
// reached bcrypt against a documented budget of 5. Namespaced, the same
// sequence gives 5 through and 35 refused.
//
// It needs one account of the attacker's own — a self-service signup on any app
// that also ran scaffold_registration, which is the pairing this tool set
// recommends.
//
// Namespacing also fixes the mirror nuisance: five bad browser logins from one
// office NAT would otherwise lock the MOBILE app for every user on that address.
//
// ─── why this is not two lines inside the handlers ───────────────────────────
//
// handlers/auth.go and handlers/mobile_auth.go are scaffold_auth output, and
// every scaffold writes with os.WriteFile — a re-run TRUNCATES them, along with
// both their _test.go files. A guard living inside the file it guards cannot
// survive the event it guards against. Keeping the keys here, with the test in
// handlers/auth_buckets_test.go, means the decision and its proof are in files
// whose whole content is this one concern: a re-run rewrites them with the same
// correct content, and a hand-edit that reverts a call site goes red. That test
// is the actual fail-loud mechanism — an unused function is not a Go compile
// error, so the file alone would not notice.
//
// Same reasoning that put clientIP in handlers/clientip.go. The customization
// left inside the two scaffolded handlers is deliberately one line each.
//
// ─── the spelling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Bare action name plus the IP. The action names are the endpoints' own last
// path segments, so the key a row carries names the route that wrote it. Any
// later endpoint that counts failures — registration, password reset, an email
// resend — gets its own function here rather than reaching for a bare IP.
//
// The IP comes from clientIP (handlers/clientip.go), which has the trusted-proxy
// notion: behind a reverse proxy a key built from RemoteAddr would be ONE bucket
// for every caller on the planet.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ─── budgets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE TWO KINDS OF BUCKET DO NOT GET THE SAME NUMBER, and that asymmetry is a
// security decision rather than tuning.
//
// A per-ADDRESS bucket is what stops a lone attacker: they must spend it before
// guessing again, and they cannot clear it without succeeding. It is also the
// one a human who mistypes actually meets, so it stays tight.
//
// A per-ACCOUNT bucket is also a per-account LOCKOUT. At threshold N, anyone
// who knows an address can deny that user their own login for 15 minutes by
// spending N requests — no account, no credentials, no luck. Set equal to the
// address budget it would be a cheap denial of service against any account an
// attacker can name, which is a poor trade for the brute-force cap it buys.
// It only has to catch DISTRIBUTED guessing at one target, which needs an
// address pool and is far rarer, so it can sit well above any legitimate user's
// reach: a real user is stopped by their own address bucket at 5, four times
// over, long before touching 20.
const (
	loginIPMaxAttempts      = 5
	loginAccountMaxAttempts = 20
)

// loginBucket is POST /api/v1/auth/login — the cookie login.
func loginBucket(ip string) string { return "login:" + ip }

// loginTokenBucket is POST /api/v1/auth/login_token — the bearer login.
func loginTokenBucket(ip string) string { return "login_token:" + ip }

// ─── the per-account bucket ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE IP BUCKETS ABOVE ARE CLEARED BY A SUCCESSFUL LOGIN, AND THAT IS A BYPASS
// ON ITS OWN. Namespacing the two endpoints stopped a success on the bearer
// login from erasing the cookie login's failures; it did nothing about a
// success on the SAME endpoint with a DIFFERENT account:
//
//	4 wrong passwords against victim@example.com  -> attempts = 4, no lock
//	1 correct login to the attacker's own account -> ClearAttempts, ROW GONE
//	4 more wrong passwords against the victim     -> attempts = 4 again
//	  ...
//
// Unbounded guessing from one address, and the limiter reports itself healthy
// the whole time because the fifth attempt is never reached. Anyone who can
// sign up — which on an app that also ran scaffold_registration is anyone — has
// the account the sequence needs.
//
// Never clearing on success was the alternative and was rejected: clearing is
// genuinely useful for a legitimate user who mistyped twice and then got it
// right, and taking it away would leave them locked out by their own success.
//
// So: a SECOND bucket, keyed on the account being attacked rather than on the
// address attacking it. Both are checked before a password is looked at, both
// are counted on failure, and a successful login clears the IP bucket and THIS
// ACCOUNT'S bucket — nothing else. The invariant holds: erasing a bucket
// requires proving the credential that bucket protects.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS, AND HOW THAT COST IS KEPT SMALL: enough failures against one
// account will lock that account's logins for fifteen minutes, whoever caused
// them. That is the standard per-account-lockout trade and there is no way to
// have the throttle without it — so the budget is set well above the address
// budget (see the constants above) rather than equal to it. A lone attacker
// exhausts their OWN address bucket long before they reach a victim's account
// bucket, so locking someone out needs an address pool rather than a loop.
//
// IT MUST NOT BECOME A USER-ENUMERATION ORACLE, and three things make sure it
// is not:
//
//  1. The bucket is derived from the SUBMITTED address, not from a user row, so
//     an address with no account has one exactly like an address with one.
//  2. Both failure paths count against it — the unknown-email path as well as
//     the wrong-password path. Counting only real accounts would make "did the
//     429 ever arrive" a membership test.
//  3. Both buckets are read BEFORE the user lookup, and the 429 is byte-for-byte
//     the answer the other bucket gives, so neither the body nor the number of
//     database reads differs by whether the account exists.
//
// loginEmailBucket is shared by the cookie and bearer logins deliberately —
// they are one credential check reached two ways, and separate account buckets
// would hand an attacker ten guesses per account instead of five.
func loginEmailBucket(email string) string { return "login_email:" + emailBucketKey(email) }

// emailBucketKey is the stable, bounded, non-reversible form of an email
// address, for use as a rate-limit key.
//
// HASHED, NOT RAW. rate_limits is written by unauthenticated callers, so a raw
// key would let anyone dictate the contents of a column — turning the table
// into a log of every address anybody probed, of unbounded length, sitting in
// the same database as the accounts. The digest is 16 bytes of SHA-256: not a
// password, so there is nothing for a work factor to buy, and collisions merely
// share a bucket.
//
// LOWERCASED AND TRIMMED FIRST, and that is load bearing rather than tidy:
// without it `Victim@example.com` and `victim@example.com` are two buckets and
// the whole control is bypassed by varying the case.
func emailBucketKey(email string) string {
	sum := sha256.Sum256([]byte(strings.ToLower(strings.TrimSpace(email))))
	return hex.EncodeToString(sum[:16])
}

// ─── using two buckets without repeating four lines per branch ───────────────

// recordLoginFailure counts one failed login against every bucket that meters
// it.
//
// The write error is NOT ignorable even though nothing useful can be said to
// the caller: a failure here means the attempt was never counted, so the
// counter never trips and the control is silently off. Every bucket is still
// attempted after one fails — a limiter that gives up halfway is a limiter that
// stopped limiting.
func recordLoginFailure(m *models.UserModel, ipBucket, accountBucket string) {
	recordAttempt(m, ipBucket, loginIPMaxAttempts)
	recordAttempt(m, accountBucket, loginAccountMaxAttempts)
}

// recordAttempt is the shared body — one bucket, one budget, error logged.
func recordAttempt(m *models.UserModel, bucket string, maxAttempts int) {
	if err := m.RecordFailedAttemptWithLimit(bucket, maxAttempts); err != nil {
		log.Printf("handlers: rate limiter failed to record an attempt for %q: %v", bucket, err)
	}
}

// clearLoginAttempts forgets the buckets a successful login is entitled to
// forget: the caller's address, and the account that just authenticated. Never
// a bucket belonging to an account the caller has not proved they hold.
func clearLoginAttempts(m *models.UserModel, buckets ...string) {
	for _, bucket := range buckets {
		if err := m.ClearAttempts(bucket); err != nil {
			log.Printf("handlers: rate limiter failed to clear %q: %v", bucket, err)
		}
	}
}

// loginLimited reports whether ANY of the given buckets is currently locked,
// writing the response itself when the answer is yes or when the read fails.
//
// A READ FAILURE IS A 500, NOT A 429 — collapsing the two makes a broken
// database tell every caller "too many attempts, try again in 15 minutes", a
// specific and false claim that sends an operator to the limiter instead of to
// the outage. Failing closed is right; lying about why is not.
//
// The bucket name reaches the log, which is another reason the account key is a
// digest: an operator gets a stable identifier to correlate on, and no address.
func loginLimited(w http.ResponseWriter, m *models.UserModel, buckets ...string) bool {
	for _, bucket := range buckets {
		locked, err := m.IsRateLimited(bucket)
		if err != nil {
			log.Printf("handlers: rate-limit read failed for %q: %v", bucket, err)
			jsonError(w, "Something went wrong. Try again.", http.StatusInternalServerError)
			return true
		}
		if locked {
			jsonError(w, "Too many attempts. Try again in 15 minutes.", http.StatusTooManyRequests)
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// registerBucket is POST /api/v1/auth/register.
//
// The banner above says any later endpoint that counts attempts gets its own
// function here rather than reaching for a bare IP; this is the first of them.
//
// Registration needed a bucket at all because it was the one unauthenticated
// write in the scaffold set with NO limit on it, and it answers 409 for an
// address that already has an account. Login goes to real trouble not to leak
// that — dummyHash exists so a missing account costs the same bcrypt time as a
// wrong password — and then registration published the same fact outright, one
// request per address, unmetered. The 409 is worth keeping (a signup form that
// cannot say "you already have an account" is a worse product), so the fix is
// to make asking expensive rather than to make the answer vague.
//
// Unlike the login buckets, EVERY attempt counts here, not only failures: the
// thing being limited is how fast one address can create accounts and probe
// addresses, and a successful signup is exactly that. Nothing clears this
// bucket; it decays on its own after the window, like every other bucket.
func registerBucket(ip string) string { return "register:" + aggregateIPv6Prefix(ip) }

// aggregateIPv6Prefix widens an IPv6 address to its /64 so every address one
// operator routes as a block shares one registration bucket. Without this,
// the register key is per-address — and IPv6 makes addresses effectively
// free: an attacker rotating interface identifiers inside a single routed
// /64 gets a fresh 5-per-15-min registration budget per address, so the
// 409 email-enumeration oracle (and account-creation itself) is throttled by
// nothing at scale. /64 is the smallest block an ISP routes as a unit, so
// distinct customers still get distinct buckets. IPv4 passes through
// unchanged — its scarcity already makes per-address meaningful.
//
// Masking rather than string surgery so allocation units BELOW /64 (/48,
// /56, /62) collapse into their /64 instead of splitting off; an
// unparseable value passes through so the limiter never mints an
// unpredictable key out of garbage.
func aggregateIPv6Prefix(ip string) string {
	parsed := net.ParseIP(strings.TrimSpace(ip))
	if parsed == nil || parsed.To4() != nil {
		return ip
	}
	return parsed.To16().Mask(net.CIDRMask(64, 128)).String()
}
